+# Daily Grind origin migration
+
+This repository originally contained only a static page, with no installed-app manifest, settings schema, or hosting credentials. The implementation prepares two deployment bundles; it does not change a live installation from this workspace.
+
+Edit `migration.config.json` with your actual origins before production. Defaults assume a move from `https://app.dailygrind.example` to `https://order.dailygrind.example`. Both must use HTTPS and share a registrable domain, including private suffix rules. `oldId` must exactly match the existing installed app's resolved manifest ID (or its historical start URL if it had no explicit ID). Never assign a new identity to the old installation during rollout. Adjust `newId` if needed; IDs are identities, while the installation page remains `/`.
+
+Set `settingsKeys` to the actual localStorage preference keys. The default is `daily-grind:settings`, since this starter has no existing configuration storage. Do not include authentication tokens or session secrets. Values are copied verbatim, up to 64 KiB per key. IndexedDB, cookies, permissions, and server account data need separate application-specific handling; this starter uses none of them. Browser notification and location permissions must be requested again at the destination.
+
+Run `npm ci`, `npm test`, and `npm run build`. No server is required for these commands.
+
+## Deployment order
+
+1. Deploy `dist/new/` at the new origin, including its manifest, icons, scripts, and configuration.
+2. Deploy `dist/old/` at the old origin. Its manifest signals `migrate_to`; its extensionless `/.well-known/web-app-origin-association` authorizes the exact new manifest ID. Serve that file as JSON, without authentication or redirects.
+3. Keep the old origin, manifest URL, installation page, and `/migration/bridge.html` available throughout the migration window. Do not blanket-redirect the old site. If an existing service worker caches these files, update its routing/cache policy to deliver the new files. Preserve the pre-existing manifest URL when integrating these bundles with a previously deployed app.
+4. Apply the generated `_headers` rules through the hosting platform if it does not support that file. Permit the new origin to frame the old bridge; a global `X-Frame-Options: DENY` or `SAMEORIGIN` header would prevent transfer. On the new site's CSP, allow `frame-src` for the exact old origin. Do not allow arbitrary framing of the bridge.
+
+Supported browsers discover the manifest handshake and offer an app update that preserves installation integration, including OS shortcuts. This uses `suggest` rather than forcing removal. [Chrome documents origin migration starting with Chrome 150](https://developer.chrome.com/blog/seamless-pwa-origin-migration). Other browsers may require installing the new app manually; automatic shortcut preservation cannot be guaranteed there. The old site presents a link and fallback guidance without removing the old installation.
+
+On first visit to the new origin, a hidden old-origin bridge transfers only allowlisted settings. Both sides check the exact peer origin and window, and the destination correlates replies with a random nonce. New-origin values take precedence; the old values are never deleted. A completion marker prevents replay on later launches. Blocked storage, unreachable old hosting, and quota errors leave the transfer retryable, with a visible retry button. Third-party storage restrictions or isolated storage partitions can require separate account synchronization; an iframe cannot bypass them. Consumers can listen for `daily-grind:settings-migrated` to refresh settings loaded before the transfer completed.
+
+Automated checks cover manifest authorization, same-site rejection, message boundaries, allowlisting, conflicts, and retry safety. No browser verification or development server is needed to build these artifacts.
